<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Candlemas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Feast of the Presentation of Jesus Christ, Feast of the Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary, Feast of the Holy Encounter, Hypapante, La Chandeleur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Candlemas is a Christian feast day, kept on 2 February, that commemorates the presentation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> at the Temple by Joseph and Mary, as told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>. It falls on what is traditionally the 40th day and the conclusion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday">Epiphany</a> season. One of the oldest feasts of the church, it was recorded by the pilgrim Egeria as celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the 380s, and it was introduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and other places from the mid-7th century. Many Christians take candles to church to be blessed and use them for the rest of the year as a symbol of Jesus Christ, the &quot;Light of the World&quot;. A German saying about a hedgehog's shadow on Candlemas was the origin of Groundhog Day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/candlemas.jpg" alt="Candlemas" width="330" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea Mantegna · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_Mantegna_049.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Candlemas</b>, on 2 February, is traditionally the fortieth day and the conclusion of the season.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Candlemas</b>, on 2 February, the fortieth day, was recorded in Jerusalem in the 380s and introduced to Rome from the mid-7th century.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
